<chapter xml:id="ch14-parametric-curves-polar-coordinates-and-conics" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Parametric Curves, Polar Coordinates, and Conics</title>

  <introduction>
  <p>Differential equations taught us to let time drive a function.  A solution might be
written as</p>

  <md>t\longmapsto y(t),</md>

  <p>where one input <m>t</m> produces one output <m>y</m>.</p>

  <p>But some curves resist being written as</p>

  <md>y=f(x).</md>

  <p>A circle fails the vertical line test.  A loop may pass above the same <m>x</m>-value more
than once.  A point on a wheel can rise, fall, and return to the same horizontal
position.</p>

  <p>The curve is not the problem.  The notation is the problem.</p>

  <p>Instead of forcing one coordinate to depend on the other, we let both coordinates
depend on a parameter:</p>

  <md>x=x(t),
\qquad
y=y(t).</md>

  <p>A moving point draws the curve.</p>
  </introduction>

